{
  "$schema": "https://turbo.build/schema.json",
  // Read by `fusion/scripts/version.mjs`, which hands the resolved release
  // version back to the workflow that asked for it. Declared so the lint that
  // watches for undeclared environment access stays meaningful.
  // **The platform is part of the hash, because turbo does not put it there and
  // the artifacts differ by it.** Measured 25 Aug 2026, the day the remote cache
  // started working: CI restored a build this laptop had written, and
  // `check:gen:metrics` then reported `@fusion.dev/helix-theme` at 566,424 bytes over 16
  // files where a Linux build produces 465,772 over 8. A check whose answer
  // depends on which machine warmed the cache is not a check.
  //
  // `RUNNER_OS` and `RUNNER_ARCH` are set by GitHub Actions and unset on a
  // desk, so the two populations never share an entry. What matters is kept:
  // every job in a run is Linux, so the check shards and the browser
  // shards still restore each other's build, which is where the saving was.
  // Nothing is lost but a laptop feeding a runner, which is the thing that
  // broke.
  //
  // A first-class platform key would be better than borrowing two environment
  // variables from one CI provider. Turborepo has none, so this is the idiom
  // rather than a preference.
  "globalEnv": ["RUNNER_ARCH", "RUNNER_OS"],
  "globalPassThroughEnv": ["GITHUB_OUTPUT", "NODE_AUTH_TOKEN"],
  "tasks": {
    // The two tiers generate `element-events.generated.ts` from their own
    // manifest before `tsc` reads it, so it is a build output and has to be
    // declared: an undeclared one is what a cache hit does not restore, and a
    // hit then reports success over a tree missing a module `index.ts` imports.
    "@fusion.dev/helix-headless#build": {
      "dependsOn": ["^build", "^analyze", "analyze", "icons", "styles"],
      "outputs": ["dist/**", "src/element-events.generated.ts"]
    },
    // `token-layer` writes `src/tokens.css` from `src/_modes.css` and the
    // registry, so the stylesheet is a build output and has to be declared here
    // as well, or a cache hit leaves `styles-dist` nothing to strip.
    "@fusion.dev/helix-styles#build": {
      "dependsOn": ["^build", "^analyze", "analyze", "icons", "styles"],
      "outputs": ["dist/**", "src/tokens.css"]
    },
    "@fusion.dev/helix#build": {
      "dependsOn": ["^build", "^analyze", "analyze", "icons", "styles"],
      "outputs": ["dist/**", "src/element-events.generated.ts"]
    },
    // **`pages.generated.ts` is written here, not read here**, and declaring it
    // as an input was the same mistake `docs` carried: a file a task writes and
    // does not declare is a file a cache hit leaves at whatever the last run put
    // there, while the generated module beside it is restored fresh. The two
    // then disagree and only `-- --check` notices.
    "@fusion.dev/insights#build": {
      "dependsOn": ["^build"],
      "outputs": ["src/pages.generated.ts", "src/sources.generated.ts"]
    },
    // `cem analyze` writes four files and this used to claim one, then three.
    // The manifest is the obvious output; `jsx.d.ts`, `vue.d.ts` and
    // `svelte.d.ts` are written beside it by the framework plugins, and an
    // output a task does not declare is one a cache hit does not restore.
    //
    // `svelte.d.ts` was the one still missing, and it was invisible while the
    // file was tracked: a cache hit left the working copy alone because git
    // already held it. Untracked, the same cache hit leaves nothing at all, and
    // `files` names it, so the package would ship without the typings a Svelte
    // consumer imports.
    // Its own `build` was in `dependsOn` and did not belong: `inputs` is
    // `src/**`, the config imports `@fusion.dev/helix-cem` which `^build`
    // already covers, and a manifest built with `dist` absent is byte-identical
    // to one built beside it, measured at 75,232 lines on `helix/headless`.
    // Removing it is what lets `build` depend on `analyze` instead, so a tier
    // can generate `element-events.generated.ts` as a prebuild step rather than
    // tracking it.
    "analyze": {
      "dependsOn": ["^build"],
      "inputs": ["src/**", "custom-elements-manifest.config.ts"],
      "outputs": [
        "custom-elements.json",
        "shared/types/jsx.d.ts",
        "shared/types/svelte.d.ts",
        "shared/types/vue.d.ts"
      ]
    },
    "build": {
      "dependsOn": ["^build", "^analyze", "analyze", "icons", "styles"],
      "inputs": ["$TURBO_DEFAULT$", "!**/*.spec.ts"],
      // **`wasm/**` was here and had to go, because a cache hit was deleting
      // tracked files.** It was declared when `@fusion.dev/helix-theme`'s build made
      // the wasm, so restoring it alongside `dist` was right. 273 tracked the
      // artifact and made that build `tsc` alone, and a declared output turbo
      // manages: the cached set for `wasm/**` is empty, so every cache hit
      // replaced four tracked files with nothing. Reproduced on 25 Aug 2026,
      // `git status` showing all four deleted, and measured in CI first as
      // `@fusion.dev/helix-theme` shipping 8 files where the tree holds 16.
      //
      // A tracked source is never a task output. `files` still publishes
      // `wasm`, `check:css:wasm` still measures it, and nothing builds it here.
      //
      // **`public/sandbox` and `public/specs` are outputs and were not declared,
      // which is the mirror of the `wasm` fault and cost four CI jobs.** The
      // site's build is `catalog && frame && specs && next build`, so a cache hit
      // skips all four and restores only what is listed: `.next` came back,
      // `frame.html` did not, and both directories are gitignored so nothing else
      // could put them there. Every browser job then failed one second in with
      // `Cannot read properties of null (reading 'querySelectorAll')`, because
      // `preview-root` lives in the frame that was never written. Red since the
      // remote cache started hitting, and never for a reason in the code.
      //
      // The `wasm` lesson was that a tracked file must not be declared. This is
      // the other half: a generated file that nothing tracks must be.
      "outputs": ["dist/**", ".next/**", "!.next/cache/**", "public/sandbox/**", "public/specs/**"]
    },
    // The fourth layer is `helix/styled/shared/css/elements.css` and it was not in
    // so editing any component stylesheet did not invalidate this. `dependsOn`
    // orders the two tasks and ordering is not invalidation: the sandbox
    // stylesheet, which is also what the browser spec runner serves as its
    // whole token layer, could sit a build behind the components it is meant to
    // paint.
    // Reads the manifests and every `.examples` file, so it depends on the
    // analyzer having run: `analyze` writes `custom-elements.json`, and a
    // catalog built before it would describe whatever the last analysis left.
    "catalog": {
      "dependsOn": ["^build", "^analyze"],
      "inputs": [
        "$TURBO_ROOT$/helix/docs/src/catalog.ts",
        "$TURBO_ROOT$/helix/docs/src/naming.ts",
        "$TURBO_ROOT$/helix/docs/content/**",
        "$TURBO_ROOT$/helix/docs/src/reference.ts",
        "content/routes/routes.ts",
        "$TURBO_ROOT$/helix/headless/custom-elements.json",
        "$TURBO_ROOT$/helix/sandbox/custom-elements.json",
        "$TURBO_ROOT$/helix/styled/custom-elements.json",
        "$TURBO_ROOT$/helix/styled/src/*/.examples/**",
        "$TURBO_ROOT$/fusion/docs/insights/**",
        "$TURBO_ROOT$/fusion/site/scripts/build-catalog.mjs"
      ],
      // **`public/insights` is an output and was not declared**, which is the
      // same fault this file records two tasks down about `public/sandbox` and
      // `public/specs`. `routes` asks whether every published path has a
      // document under `public/insights`, so a cache hit restored a tree
      // without them and the check reported six essays as having no document.
      // The insights content is an input for the other half of it: six essays
      // were added and the cache key did not move, so the task that writes
      // those documents was never asked to run again.
      //
      // The set is `.gitignore`'s, which is the list of what this task
      // generates and the only place it was written down: `public/*.md`,
      // `public/helix/*.md`, `public/helix/components/`, `public/helix/docs/`
      // and `public/insights/`. Declaring them one failure at a time found
      // `insights` and then `helix/*.md` on the next run, because a `.md` is
      // written per route and a route is as deep as its path.
      "outputs": [
        "content/catalog/catalog.generated.json",
        "public/*.md",
        "public/helix/components/**",
        "public/helix/docs/**",
        "public/helix/*.md",
        "public/insights/**"
      ]
    },
    "content": {
      "inputs": ["insights/content/**", "$TURBO_ROOT$/fusion/docs/scripts/build-content.mjs"],
      "outputs": ["src/pages.generated.ts", "src/sources.generated.ts"]
    },

    "dev": {
      "cache": false,
      "persistent": true
    },

    // **Not cached, and it took a stale cache hit to see why.** This writes the
    // token tables back into the `content/*.mdx` it reads, so its inputs are
    // also its outputs: a run changes the key it would have been stored under,
    // and the next hit restores the two generated modules without the mdx edits
    // that produced them. The tree then holds a generated file and a source that
    // disagree, and `-- --check` says stale while the plain task reports
    // success from a replayed log. `samples` has the same shape and the same
    // answer.
    "docs": { "cache": false },

    // **Four that cannot cache, each for its own reason, said rather than
    // discovered.** `faces` fetches a font list over the network, so a cache hit
    // would pin whatever Google answered the first time. `peers` installs four
    // component libraries into a scratch directory and weighs them. `timings`
    // measures `performance.now()`, which is not a function of its inputs.
    // `wasm-check` derives seventeen themes through the tracked binary, which is
    // the artifact it exists to distrust.
    "faces": { "cache": false },
    "frame": {
      "dependsOn": ["^build"],
      "inputs": [
        "preview/preview-frame-entry.ts",
        "preview/preview-frame.css",
        "public/fonts/**",
        "$TURBO_ROOT$/helix/styles/src/*.css",
        "$TURBO_ROOT$/helix/styled/shared/css/elements.css",
        "$TURBO_ROOT$/fusion/site/scripts/build-frame.mjs"
      ],
      "outputs": ["public/sandbox/**"]
    },
    "icons": {
      "inputs": ["icons.txt", "raw/**", "$TURBO_ROOT$/helix/icons/scripts/build-icons.mjs"],
      "outputs": ["src/generated.ts"]
    },
    "peers": { "cache": false },

    // Reads the manifest, so it waits for whoever writes one.

    // **Three more that declare a task without caching it, which is a real
    // answer rather than a gap.** `tokens` counts every read of every token
    // across two whole domains, so its key changes on nearly every commit and a
    // cache would cost a hash to miss. `routes` is the same shape one step over.
    // `samples` writes back into the files it reads, so its own output changes
    // the key it was stored under and the next run always misses. Declared
    // anyway, because `turbo run` needs the declaration and because a task in
    // the graph can be depended on.
    // The register the Rust door reads, generated from the TypeScript one the
    // JavaScript door imports. Not cached: it is one file from one file and the
    // check is cheaper than a cache lookup.
    "register": { "cache": false },
    "routes": { "cache": false },
    "samples": { "cache": false },

    "specs": {
      "dependsOn": ["^build"],
      "inputs": [
        "$TURBO_ROOT$/helix/headless/src/**/*.dom.spec.ts",
        "$TURBO_ROOT$/helix/styled/src/**/*.dom.spec.ts",
        "$TURBO_ROOT$/helix/testing/src/browser.ts",
        "$TURBO_ROOT$/fusion/site/scripts/build-specs.mjs"
      ],
      "outputs": ["public/specs/**"]
    },
    // Two generators, two outputs. `build-styles.mjs` writes the `.css.ts`
    // mirrors and `build-elements-css.mjs` writes `elements.css` at the package
    // root, and only the first was declared. Measured: delete `elements.css`
    // with a warm cache and `turbo run styles` prints "2 successful, 2 total"
    // while the file stays missing, because a cache hit restores what a task
    // claims and nothing else. The next thing to read it fails, which is how
    // this was found: `build-frame.mjs` on ENOENT.
    "styles": {
      "inputs": [
        "src/**/*.css",
        "$TURBO_ROOT$/fusion/scripts/build-styles.mjs",
        "$TURBO_ROOT$/fusion/scripts/build-elements-css.mjs"
      ],
      "outputs": ["src/**/*.css.ts", "shared/css/elements.css", "shared/css/*.css.ts"]
    },

    "styles-dist": {
      "inputs": ["src/**", "$TURBO_ROOT$/helix/styles/scripts/build-styles-dist.mjs"],
      "outputs": ["dist/**"]
    },
    "test": {
      "dependsOn": ["^build"],
      "inputs": ["src/**", "tests/**"],
      "outputs": []
    },

    // Renders React to markup, so it reads the pages it renders.
    //
    // **A wildcard segment where the route group is**, because a route group is
    // written in parentheses and parentheses are alternation to a glob parser:
    // the literal path fails to parse rather than matching nothing, which is the
    // better of the two ways for that to go wrong.
    "themed-page": {
      "dependsOn": ["^build"],
      "inputs": ["app/*/helix/theme/**", "$TURBO_ROOT$/fusion/site/scripts/build-themed-page.mjs"],
      "outputs": ["app/*/helix/theme/themed-page-markup.generated.ts"]
    },
    "timings": { "cache": false },

    // **The generators, one task each, and the reason is the cache rather than
    // the spelling.** Each of these was a root alias reading `--filter <pkg>
    // <script>`, which ran every time it was called; eight of them run in
    // `verify`. Declared here they answer from cache when nothing they read has
    // moved, and `turbo run <task> -- --check` passes the flag through and into
    // the hash, so the generate and the compare are one task rather than two
    // aliases.
    //
    // **An output not declared is one a cache hit does not restore**, which was
    // invisible while these artifacts were tracked and is not any more. Every
    // `outputs` below names everything its script writes.

    // `src/tokens.ts` is the whole input: the layer is spelled there and this
    // writes both the stylesheet block and the registry the docs read.
    // **Uncached, because one of its outputs is partly written by hand.**
    //
    // `build-token-layer.mjs` replaces what sits between two markers in
    // `src/tokens.css` and preserves everything outside them, which the file
    // relies on: the mode declarations and the reduced-motion block are
    // authored there and say so. Turbo has no way to express half a file, so
    // declaring the whole thing as an output meant a cache hit restored the
    // cached copy over anything hand-written since. Reproduced 31 Aug 2026: a
    // block appended after the closing marker survived the generator executing
    // and vanished on the next run reporting FULL TURBO.
    //
    // Adding the file to its own `inputs` would fix the hit and make every run
    // a miss, which is the same cost with a worse name. This task takes about
    // 200ms, so the cache was never buying much. Same fault as `wasm/**` in
    // 273, and the third answer there was the same: stop declaring it.
    "token-layer": {
      "cache": false,
      "inputs": [
        "src/_modes.css",
        "src/tokens.ts",
        "$TURBO_ROOT$/helix/styles/scripts/build-token-layer.mjs"
      ],
      "outputs": ["src/tokens.css", "dist/tokens.json"]
    },
    // **`^analyze` and not only `^build`, because the typings are analyzer
    // output.** `jsx.d.ts` is emitted by `analyze` and is gitignored, so on a
    // clean checkout there is nothing on disk for the site's
    // `import '@fusion.dev/helix-sandbox/jsx'` to contribute: every custom
    // element written in JSX becomes an unknown `JSX.IntrinsicElements` member.
    // It passed locally the whole time, because a developer who has ever run
    // `analyze` has the file. Four errors across two site files, and the job
    // that catches it runs `bun install` and `typecheck` and nothing between.
    "typecheck": {
      "dependsOn": ["^build", "^analyze"],
      "inputs": ["$TURBO_DEFAULT$"],
      "outputs": []
    },
    "wasm-check": { "cache": false }
  },
  "ui": "stream"
}
